<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">Kingdom of Kent</h1><p class="st-meta">455 CE – 871 CE (approx.) · capital Canterbury</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first English kingdom to accept Christianity, under Æthelberht in 597, and the home of the earliest laws written in English.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bede">Bede</a> says Kent was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hengist-and-horsa">Hengest and Horsa</a>, who came as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenaries</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vortigern">Vortigern</a> around 449 and then turned against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britons-celtic-people">Britons</a>. The story is legendary, but archaeology shows strong links with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a> and Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> by the 6th century. The kingdom took its name from the British <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cantiaci">Cantiaci</a>, and its centre was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aethelberht-of-kent">Æthelberht</a>, king from about 589 to 616, married the Frankish Christian princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bertha-of-kent">Bertha</a> and in 597 received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustine-of-canterbury">Augustine</a>'s mission from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-gregory-i">Pope Gregory</a>, making <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canterbury">Canterbury</a> the seat of the English Church. His law code is the earliest document in English. Kent was rich from trade with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Francia</a>, producing fine garnet jewellery and gold coinage, and later kings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wihtred-of-kent">Wihtred</a> issued further laws.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>From the 760s Kent fell under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/offa-of-mercia">Offa of Mercia</a>; a revolt by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eadberht-praen">Eadberht Præn</a> was crushed in 798. In 825 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egbert-of-wessex">Egbert of Wessex</a> took Kent, which was ruled as a sub-kingdom by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wessex">West Saxon</a> princes until it was merged into <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Wessex</a> in the 860s, later becoming an English shire.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/kingdom-of-kent.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Kent" width="320" height="241"><figcaption>Image: Hel-hama · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kingdom_of_Kent.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>455 CE – 871 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Canterbury</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Æthelberht, Eadbald, Wihtred</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglo-Saxon paganism; Christianity from 597</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-kingdom">United Kingdom</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kent, Cantware</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=663&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kent" aria-label="Kingdom of Kent on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4704 401 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Kingdom of Kent lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5024 657L4962 619M5024 657L4970 663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4962 619h0M4970 663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5024 657h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5024 657h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Canterbury. <a href="/atlas?year=663&amp;focus=kingdom-of-kent">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
